WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.900
A real-estate client wants photos of a warehouse two miles from a towered airport.

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:11.640
The map app shows the building, the controller shows a clear launch area, and the drone can easily stay below 200 feet.

3
00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:13.260
That is not enough.

4
00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:24.560
The remote pilot must identify the airspace at the actual site, determine whether the operation is inside controlled airspace that requires authorization, and check current restrictions before flight.

5
00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:32.120
From a remote pilot's perspective, the National Airspace System is a set of operating environments with different rules and risks.

6
00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:46.700
Class B, C, and D airspace and the lateral boundaries of the surface area of Class E airspace designated for an airport require prior Air Traffic Control authorization for Part 107 operations.

7
00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:56.480
Class G airspace does not require that authorization under section 107.41, but it still requires compliance with all other operating rules.

8
00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:04.580
Class E is especially easy to misunderstand because it can begin at different altitudes and may have surface areas associated with airports.

9
00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:09.000
Airspace classes cannot be reduced to one universal altitude rule.

10
00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:12.000
Sectional charts help the pilot see that structure.

11
00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:28.420
Blue and magenta airport symbols, shaded controlled-airspace boundaries, dashed surface areas, special-use areas, altitude notations, heliports, seaplane bases, obstacles, and nearby navigational features all provide context.

12
00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:45.560
A remote pilot does not need to become an airline navigator to conduct a small UAS job, but the pilot must understand enough to distinguish towered and non-towered airports, runway orientation, approach and departure paths, and areas where crewed aircraft may be low and busy.

13
00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:54.300
Heliports and hospital pads deserve the same respect because helicopters may use routes and altitudes that surprise a drone crew.

14
00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:57.140
UAS Facility Maps are planning tools.

15
00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:05.080
They show the maximum altitudes around airports where the FAA may authorize Part 107 operations without additional safety analysis.

16
00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:08.220
A grid value is not automatic permission to fly.

17
00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:10.240
It informs the request.

18
00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:22.420
If LAANC, the Low Altitude Authorization and Notification Capability, is available at the airport, the pilot may submit a near-real-time request at or below eligible grid altitudes.

19
00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:31.640
A Part 107 pilot may also request further coordination when an operation needs an altitude above the UAS Facility Map ceiling, up to 400 feet.

20
00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:41.100
Where LAANC is not available or the mission does not fit LAANC, the pilot uses the FAA's manual authorization process, typically through DroneZone.

21
00:02:41.380 --> 00:02:42.820
Consider the warehouse mission.

22
00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:53.160
The site lies near the edge of a Class D surface area, with a LAANC grid showing 100 feet over part of the property and 0 feet over the parking lot closest to the runway approach path.

23
00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:56.220
The client asks for 180-foot photos.

24
00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:03.200
The remote pilot must separate requested altitude, authorized altitude, and actual operational altitude.

25
00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:14.420
Even if authorization is granted, the pilot still must preserve visual line of sight, avoid people and vehicles, maintain cloud clearance and visibility, consider obstacles, and watch for airport traffic.

26
00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:20.680
Authorization is not a waiver of other rules and not a finding that the specific mission is safe.

27
00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:25.760
Temporary restrictions can change the answer after planning is complete.

28
00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:40.420
Notices to Air Missions and Temporary Flight Restrictions may protect emergency response, wildfire aircraft, security events, stadium activities, presidential movement, national-security sites, airshows, or other time-sensitive operations.

29
00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:45.720
Prohibited and restricted areas may require permission from the using or controlling agency.

30
00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:53.420
A previously issued authorization does not eliminate the need to check current NOTAMs, TFRs, and local conditions before launch.

31
00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:59.440
The operational habit is to treat airspace review as a live decision, not paperwork finished yesterday.
